# Data Visualization With Python

These tutorials build statistical graphics in Python using [Matplotlib](https://matplotlib.org/) and [seaborn](https://seaborn.pydata.org/). Rather than demonstrating each chart type in isolation, the lessons follow a single analysis from first look to finished figure, using publicly available health and nutrition data.

Python offers two complementary approaches to plotting, and the distinction causes more confusion than any other aspect of the ecosystem. Matplotlib provides explicit, low-level control over every element of a figure. Seaborn sits on top of Matplotlib and produces statistical graphics from a data frame in a single call, returning Matplotlib objects that can then be adjusted directly. These tutorials use both deliberately and make clear which is doing the work at each step.

## Who These Tutorials Are For

Readers should be comfortable with basic Python syntax and with pandas data frames — selecting columns, filtering rows, and grouping. No prior plotting experience is assumed. Researchers in the health sciences will recognize the datasets, but the techniques apply to any field.

For help setting up Python, see [Which Python Setup Is Right for Me?](../../ai/python/10-which-python-setup.md).

## Data

Every lesson draws on the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES), a public-domain survey conducted by the United States Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Two small teaching datasets supplement it where a simpler example is clearer. [Setup and the Data](00-setup-and-data.md) describes all three, explains how the survey is structured, and covers the sampling issues that affect how its figures should be interpreted.

A parallel set of tutorials covers the [same material in R](../r/index.md).

